  "dom_id": "step-q156054",
  "name": "Question 3",
  "problem": "Solve the inequality $\\frac{3z+1}{2}>\\frac{-28+6z}{7}.$",
  "worked_solution": "First, we clear the rational expressions by multiplying both sides of the inequality by the lowest common positive multiple of the denominators:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3z+1}{2} & >\\frac{-28+6z}{7} \\\\ 14\\cdot (\\frac{3z+1}{2}) & >14\\cdot (\\frac{-28+6z}{7}) \\\\ 7\\cdot (3z+1) & >2\\cdot (-28+6z) \\\\ 21z+7 & >-56+12z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the addition and multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}21z+7 & >-56+12z \\\\ 21z+7-12z & >-56+12z-12z \\\\ 9z+7 & >-56 \\\\ 9z+7-7 & >-56-7 \\\\ 9z & >-63 \\\\ \\frac{9z}{9} & >\\frac{-63}{9} \\\\ z & >-7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $z>-7.$",
